WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.024
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.324 --> 00:00:09.722
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.022 --> 00:00:16.703
Unit seven: Media & Politics. Today you will distance yourself from unproven claims with hedging verbs.

4
00:00:17.003 --> 00:00:19.643
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.961
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:26.201
Political coverage.

7
00:00:27.217 --> 00:00:31.039
Political coverage means news reports about government and politicians.

8
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.741
Political coverage intensifies before every election.

9
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.536
Your turn. Say: Political coverage.

10
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.784
Political coverage.

11
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:44.609
Press freedom.

12
00:00:45.617 --> 00:00:50.194
Press freedom means the right of journalists to publish without government control.

13
00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:53.236
Press freedom is a sign of a healthy democracy.

14
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.851
Your turn. Say: Press freedom.

15
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.701
Press freedom.

16
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:02.683
Media bias.

17
00:01:03.683 --> 00:01:07.657
Media bias means unfair preference for one side in news reporting.

18
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.865
Media bias can shape how voters think.

19
00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.230
Your turn. Say: Media bias.

20
00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:18.288
Media bias.

21
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.190
Political spin.

22
00:01:21.183 --> 00:01:25.029
Political spin means presenting facts in a way that favours one side.

23
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:28.731
The press office used political spin to hide the loss.

24
00:01:29.317 --> 00:01:32.050
Your turn. Say: Political spin.

25
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.354
Political spin.

26
00:01:37.117 --> 00:01:39.012
Fact-based reporting.

27
00:01:40.017 --> 00:01:44.895
Fact-based reporting means news that relies on checked evidence, not opinion.

28
00:01:45.483 --> 00:01:48.145
Fact-based reporting builds public trust.

29
00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.635
Your turn. Say: Fact-based reporting.

30
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.732
Fact-based reporting.

31
00:01:57.483 --> 00:01:58.670
Public opinion.

32
00:01:59.683 --> 00:02:03.401
Public opinion means the views held by most people about an issue.

33
00:02:04.017 --> 00:02:06.759
Public opinion changed after the investigation.

34
00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:10.304
Your turn. Say: Public opinion.

35
00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:14.377
Public opinion.

36
00:02:15.117 --> 00:02:16.245
News cycle.

37
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:21.154
News cycle means the way stories rise and fade as news is reported.

38
00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.562
A fast news cycle rewards quick headlines.

39
00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:27.744
Your turn. Say: News cycle.

40
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.240
News cycle.

41
00:02:32.983 --> 00:02:34.704
Journalistic ethics.

42
00:02:35.717 --> 00:02:39.133
Journalistic ethics means the moral rules that guide reporters.

43
00:02:39.717 --> 00:02:43.028
Journalistic ethics require reporters to check sources.

44
00:02:43.617 --> 00:02:46.582
Your turn. Say: Journalistic ethics.

45
00:02:49.433 --> 00:02:51.027
Journalistic ethics.

46
00:02:51.833 --> 00:02:55.414
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:02.397
Visal, a report says 'The minister appears to have misled the public.' Why 'appears to have', not 'misled'?

48
00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:18.665
It is distancing. The writer does not state it as fact. We use seems to, appears to, is alleged to, is reported to, so-called or supposedly, so the claim is attributed, not owned.

49
00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.109
Is that part of journalistic ethics?

50
00:03:23.217 --> 00:03:34.837
Yes. Fact-based reporting separates what is known from what is claimed. Compare 'The policy failed' with 'The policy appears to have failed, according to early figures.'

51
00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:37.973
How does it help me spot spin?

52
00:03:39.083 --> 00:03:49.212
Notice who is speaking. A so-called independent poll, an alleged leak or a supposedly neutral article should make you ask for the evidence.

53
00:03:50.367 --> 00:03:51.995
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:57.439
Grammar focus: Hedging: appears to and is alleged to and so-called.

55
00:03:57.739 --> 00:03:59.053
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.214
The minister appears to have misled the public.

57
00:04:03.117 --> 00:04:06.440
The company is alleged to have paid for favourable coverage.

58
00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:10.661
The so-called independent poll seems to favour one party.

59
00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:19.625
Use appears to / seems to / is alleged to / is reported to + verb (have + past participle for earlier events) to show you are not claiming it as certain fact.

60
00:04:20.383 --> 00:04:24.069
Be careful! Don't say: The firm alleges to have lied.

61
00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:28.509
Say: The firm is alleged to have lied.

62
00:04:28.809 --> 00:04:31.377
Use the passive: is alleged to.

63
00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:34.807
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.170
Reading Between the Lines.

65
00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.443
Political coverage rarely presents the whole picture in a single story.

66
00:04:42.433 --> 00:04:48.949
Reporters following good journalistic ethics use phrases such as 'is alleged to' or 'appears to' when a claim is unproven.

67
00:04:49.933 --> 00:04:56.229
Media bias, by contrast, often hides inside words, so a so-called expert may be only a supporter.

68
00:04:57.233 --> 00:05:03.099
Political spin tries to shape public opinion during a fast news cycle, when few people check the facts.

69
00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:08.828
Press freedom and fact-based reporting therefore protect readers as much as writers.

70
00:05:09.933 --> 00:05:13.796
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:16.917
The minister blank to have misled the public.

72
00:05:17.217 --> 00:05:21.021
A, appears. B, looks like. C, is appearing.

73
00:05:24.567 --> 00:05:26.497
The answer is A: appears.

74
00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:31.159
Careful reporting that separates fact from claim is blank reporting.

75
00:05:31.459 --> 00:05:35.344
A, spin. B, fact-based. C, cycle.

76
00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.329
The answer is B: fact-based.

77
00:05:42.483 --> 00:05:44.820
A claim made but not yet proved is blank.

78
00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:48.309
A, certain. B, admitted. C, alleged.

79
00:05:51.867 --> 00:05:53.611
The answer is C: alleged.

80
00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:56.436
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:56.736 --> 00:06:03.396
A, The firm is alleged to have lied.. B, The firm alleges to have lied.. C, The firm is alleged have lied..

82
00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:09.815
The answer is A: The firm is alleged to have lied..

83
00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:14.238
According to the reading, spin tries to shape public blank.

84
00:06:14.538 --> 00:06:17.959
A, opinion. B, freedom. C, ethics.

85
00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:23.697
The answer is A: opinion.

86
00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.377
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:29.217 --> 00:06:32.760
How can a citizen recognize political spin when reading the news?

88
00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.143
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:43.443 --> 00:07:10.296
A citizen can spot spin by checking who is speaking and what they gain. Look for emotional words, selective facts and claims with no source. Compare several outlets, especially those with different viewpoints, and check the original figures where possible. Reports that separate fact from opinion and admit uncertainty are usually more reliable than confident, one-sided stories.

90
00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:12.363
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:19.234
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and hedging: appears to and is alleged to and so-called.

92
00:07:19.534 --> 00:07:24.346
Today's value is media literacy. Ask who made the message, and why, before you believe it.

93
00:07:24.646 --> 00:07:27.345
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

94
00:07:27.645 --> 00:07:30.239
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

